Don't worry if they don't make sense now. They will shortly as you go through this course.

30
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,020
These slides are in alphabetical order.

31
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:44,312
You can download the glossary of the terms in a

32
00:01:44,312 --> 00:01:47,490
PDF form to print out by visiting this

33
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:49,476
page, orderflows.com backslash glossary.

34
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:54,160
Now, all these terms, like I said, they're in alphabetical order, so they're easy to find.

35
00:01:54,680 --> 00:02:01,120
Some terms have different names, but they have the same meaning. But you'll see that as we go through.

36
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,740
Okay. Aggressive buyers. Aggressive buyers are buyers who buy from the offer.

37
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:11,240
The market will be bid and offered all the time.

38
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:14,800
An aggressive buyer is someone that buys the offer.

39
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:25,080
So, for example, at this price, $19.08, the market is $19.08 bid offered at $19.08 and a quarter.

40
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:28,800
So, the market is trading back and forth.

41
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:34,980
Whoever is buying the $19.08s and a quarter, those are considered the aggressive buyers.

42
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:40,060
If someone was selling into the $19.08 bid, you'd consider that aggressive sellers.

43
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:48,250
So, the next one is aggressive sellers. The sellers are who sell into the bid.

44
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:52,896
So, as I was saying before, people that are selling into this

45
00:02:52,896 --> 00:02:55,219
$19.08 bid, these 49 lots that traded here,

46
00:02:55,219 --> 00:02:57,155
those are the aggressive sellers.

47
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:04,020
Delta. Ask Delta. The

48
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:06,860
Delta, sorry, the total amount of volume traded

49
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:11,120
on the offer in a bar, the amount bought by aggressive buyers.

50
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,208
So, you have down here, you can see

51
00:03:14,208 --> 00:03:17,296
on some charts, on some software, they'll show

52
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:18,840
you what the Ask

53
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:23,858
Delta is and what the Bid Delta is, the Delta, the Max Delta.

54
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:25,700
There's a lot of different types of Deltas.

55
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,934
One of them is the Ask Delta. And

56
00:03:28,934 --> 00:03:31,788
what it is, it's the volume that's traded

57
00:03:31,788 --> 00:03:34,999
on the offer, what was bought from the offer.

58
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:43,980
When the market was $19.15 at $19.15 and a quarter, $1,666 lots traded on the offer.

59
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:47,630
So, you take the volume that's traded on

60
00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:51,280
the Ask side versus what's traded on the

61
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:54,018
Bid side, you'll get the Delta.

62
00:03:54,560 --> 00:04:00,200
But, all the volume that's traded on the Ask side, that is the Ask Delta.

63
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:05,080
If you were to add up all these numbers here, you'd get $17.831.

64
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:12,740
Bid Delta is opposite of the Ask Delta.

65
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:18,300
Again, it'll show up down here with your Delta numbers. It's all the volume that's traded on the Bid side.

66
00:04:24,990 --> 00:04:28,430
Okay, an average ratio number, a.k.a. a normal ratio number.

67
00:04:29,750 --> 00:04:35,830
Now, OrderFlowsTrader is the only software that has ratios.

68
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:40,130
So, if you're not using OrderFlowsTrader, this really won't make much sense to you.

69
00:04:40,970 --> 00:04:42,768
But, a ratio number that is black color

70
00:04:42,768 --> 00:04:45,689
as opposed to a blue number indicates that the ratio is just normal.

71
00:04:46,890 --> 00:04:49,130
A regular ratio without price rejection or absorption.

72
00:04:50,370 --> 00:04:55,150
You're going to see two types of numbers on the top of a bar or below a bar.

73
00:04:55,310 --> 00:04:56,910
If it's a red bar, it'll be on top.

74
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:59,810
If it's a green bar, it'll be on the bottom.

75
00:05:00,150 --> 00:05:02,950
So, in this case, you have a black number here.

76
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:07,550
You have a blue number here. The black number is an average ratio number.

77
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:13,892
It's one that doesn't meet the requirements of indicating that there's a

78
00:05:13,892 --> 00:05:18,649
lack of follow-through on a move or price rejection.

79
00:05:18,650 --> 00:05:22,130
Here, you have a 90.5.

80
00:05:22,210 --> 00:05:24,270
Here, you have 2.067.

81
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:27,290
I'll get into it later, what the ratios mean.

82
00:05:27,790 --> 00:05:30,390
But, for now, we'll just talk about the colors.

83
00:05:30,750 --> 00:05:32,470
An average ratio number is going to be black.

84
00:05:32,470 --> 00:05:38,242
A ratio that's in blue will indicate something like either

85
00:05:38,242 --> 00:05:45,169
strong offers, strong bids, or lack of buying or lack of selling.

86
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:49,620
Next one is a buying imbalance.

87
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,463
A buying imbalance occurs when buyers are aggressively buying from the offer

88
00:05:53,463 --> 00:05:55,611
at a ratio of 400% or greater relative

89
00:05:55,611 --> 00:05:57,452
to what's sold on the bid.

90
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:01,400
So, down here, you have this buying imbalance.

91
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:10,280
You have 49 against 263. The 263 is in blue indicating a buying imbalance.

92
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:18,740
You're taking the ratio of 49 against 263. It's over 400%. That's why it's showing up as a buying imbalance.

93
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:29,740
Here, you have 134 against 358. It's not a buying imbalance because 134 into 358 is less than 400%.

94
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:40,390
The commitment of traders in order flows context, it is the price in a bar where the most volume occurs.

95
00:06:41,670 --> 00:06:48,250
So, on most order flows software, they'll put a box around the commitment of traders.

96
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:55,570
It's also called, you know, point of control. You hear the term POC, COT, point of control, commitment of traders.

97
00:06:56,270 --> 00:06:59,350
In order flow context, on a footprint chart, they all have the same meaning.

98
00:07:00,170 --> 00:07:02,390
Some people like to refer to it as COT.

99
00:07:02,590 --> 00:07:04,830
Others like to refer to it as point of control.

100
00:07:05,990 --> 00:07:10,690
And what it's telling you is where in the bar the most volume appeared.

101
00:07:11,190 --> 00:07:15,130
So, you see here, you got 4614 and 4414.

102
00:07:15,690 --> 00:07:17,462
It just takes the price level.

103
00:07:17,462 --> 00:07:19,234
It's not measuring it diagonally, what's

104
00:07:19,234 --> 00:07:21,006
traded against the bid, what's traded

105
00:07:21,006 --> 00:07:24,847
on the offer. It's taking the price level where the most volume occurred.

106
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:31,600
Cumulative delta is the accumulation of delta over the entire trading session.

107
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,900
You'll see down here, cumulative delta.

108
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:38,987
So, each bar will have a delta of either 0,

109
00:07:38,987 --> 00:07:41,445
which is rare, or a positive

110
00:07:41,445 --> 00:07:47,590
or negative number. And it's taking the cumulative number since the beginning of the session.

111
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:54,140
So, say it's a session that's been rallying. Generally, you'll have a positive cumulative delta.

112
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:58,440
If you're in a session that's been selling off all

113
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:02,819
day long, most often you should have a negative cumulative delta.

114
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:09,220
Cumulative volume is the accumulation of volume over the entire trading session.

115
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,933
It's going to be on the bottom here

116
00:08:11,933 --> 00:08:14,646
with NinjaTrader. They put their copyright logo right

117
00:08:14,646 --> 00:08:16,681
over that. So, you can't really

118
00:08:16,681 --> 00:08:26,518
see it, or at least the label. You can see the numbers, but you can't see that it says cumulative volume. And what that is, it's the day's volume.

119
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Why is that important?

120
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,580
Well, you can judge if the market is fast or slow. I mean, busy or not by the cumulative delta.

121
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:39,900
You know, by certain times of the day, you should have reached a certain amount of volume.

122
00:08:40,659 --> 00:08:43,539
On slow days or holidays, you'll notice that

123
00:08:43,539 --> 00:08:48,579
the volume is much lighter. And you can see that in the cumulative volume.

124
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:51,618
Like you say, you know, by 930, you should already

125
00:08:51,618 --> 00:08:54,140
have a half a million contracts in the

126
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:57,924
E-minis have traded. If it's much less, then the volume is light.

127
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:01,251
The delta.

128
00:09:01,251 --> 00:09:04,259
The delta is the difference in a bar between the

129
00:09:04,259 --> 00:09:07,869
volume traded on the offer minus the volume traded on the bid.

130
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:13,230
Now, you see also later positive delta and negative delta in the definitions.

131
00:09:13,230 --> 00:09:16,250
And delta is very important.

132
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:20,670
It's something that not a lot of people look at at all.

133
00:09:21,570 --> 00:09:25,830
I mean, basically only order flow traders really spend any time with delta.

134
00:09:26,710 --> 00:09:28,290
And, you know, why is it important?

135
00:09:28,450 --> 00:09:31,182
On bars that are up, you

136
00:09:31,182 --> 00:09:33,914
know, bars that open and then

137
00:09:33,914 --> 00:09:37,557
close higher. You know, if a bar opens

138
00:09:37,557 --> 00:09:41,656
down here at 1915.25, closes at 1917.25, it's an up bar.

139
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:44,590
It should have positive delta.

140
00:09:47,490 --> 00:09:56,010
So you're taking what's traded, the ask delta minus the bid delta to get the total delta for the bar.

141
00:09:56,490 --> 00:10:02,850
So in an up move, you know, when a market's rallying, you should string together positive deltas in each bar.

142
00:10:03,290 --> 00:10:08,350
There will be bars that are, you know, red bars, negative bars that do have a positive delta.

143
00:10:08,350 --> 00:10:12,930
And there will be green bars that do have negative delta.

144
00:10:13,670 --> 00:10:16,590
You know, delta is a function of the volume that's traded inside the bar.

145
00:10:17,630 --> 00:10:19,930
It's important, you know, I'll explain later.

146
00:10:20,030 --> 00:10:25,610
I have a whole chapter, a whole lesson developed around delta and its importance.

147
00:10:25,610 --> 00:10:31,250
But, in general, on a move up, you should be having positive deltas.

148
00:10:31,970 --> 00:10:34,690
And on a move down, you should be having negative deltas.

149
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:38,780
Divergence, in order flow terms,

150
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,657
is when a market makes a new

151
00:10:40,657 --> 00:10:43,338
or equal high and the bar has a negative delta.

152
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:48,820
Or when a market makes a new or equal low and the bar has positive delta.

153
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:55,060
Basically, price is acting different than an indicator. In this case, the indicator is the delta.

154
00:10:55,520 --> 00:11:00,300
So if you're making a high and you have negative delta, technically that's a divergence.

155
00:11:05,170 --> 00:11:08,050
People also refer to it as delta divergence.

156
00:11:08,670 --> 00:11:10,532
It's usually marked with a triangle

157
00:11:10,532 --> 00:11:12,394
on a chart. I mean, most

158
00:11:12,394 --> 00:11:15,808
are, depending on some charts. Some charts don't even show it.

159
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:16,930
Why is that important?

160
00:11:16,930 --> 00:11:19,916
Well, you hit the high and there's a

161
00:11:19,916 --> 00:11:22,156
lot of selling in there causing

162
00:11:22,156 --> 00:11:24,769
a negative delta. That's important to know.

163
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:27,410
You're not going to necessarily see that on just a normal bar chart.

164
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:31,070
So if you can see, you know, markets rallying, rallying, rallying.

165
00:11:31,110 --> 00:11:33,647
Then you hit the high and all of a sudden

166
00:11:33,647 --> 00:11:35,677
sellers come in and overwhelm the buyers. And

167
00:11:35,677 --> 00:11:39,229
you've got a big negative delta like you have here. The market should react.

168
00:11:40,330 --> 00:11:45,430
Now, in a market that keeps rallying all day, you will get more and more deltas.

169
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:50,610
You know, just as maybe the market starts to pull back. You should take everything into context.

170
00:11:51,310 --> 00:11:56,750
I get into a little bit more with reversal signals later in the course.

171
00:11:58,450 --> 00:12:01,530
Footprint chart. This is a chart used by OrderFlowsTrader.

172
00:12:02,070 --> 00:12:06,113
Footprint chart is the generic term that most OrderFlows software

173
00:12:06,113 --> 00:12:11,369
uses to break down the volume traded on the bid and the offer.

174
00:12:11,370 --> 00:12:19,520
It provides the price, the bid traded volume, and the offer traded volume together.

175
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:27,240
So you can see what traded on the bid, what traded on the offer, and know how it compares with each other.

176
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:31,972
And as the market's moving, it's called a footprint chart because, you

177
00:12:31,972 --> 00:12:36,384
know, imagine an animal walking through the mud, right? It leaves a

178
00:12:36,384 --> 00:12:43,738
footprint just as traders are trading through the market. They're leaving their footprint in the market as measured by volume.

179
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:46,470
So imbalance on a high.

180
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:50,519
Imbalance on a high occurs when there's a buying imbalance on the top of a red bar.

181
00:12:51,380 --> 00:12:56,020
What this means is it's a sell signal. You want to trade in the opposite direction.

182
00:12:56,580 --> 00:12:58,580
Some people will call it trap

183
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:01,246
traders. Trap traders, I don't particularly like to

184
00:13:01,246 --> 00:13:03,912
use that term, trap traders, just because most

185
00:13:03,912 --> 00:13:09,578
markets are pretty liquid that, you know, these guys, they're trapped. They can get out pretty easily.

186
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:13,760
But why is it important is imbalance on a high.

187
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:19,520
Here you have the blue number, which is indicating a buying imbalance. So you got 221 against 1045.

188
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:24,380
So it's a ratio of, you know, 400% more that's traded on the offer.

189
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:27,843
But why didn't the market rally keep going? And you

190
00:13:27,843 --> 00:13:30,181
have a lot of aggressive buyers, people trying

191
00:13:30,181 --> 00:13:34,858
to buy the breakout, but they can't push it any further. It just stops right there.

192
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:40,240
You know, you want to take it in context with where the market is.

193
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:45,780
You know, if it's just an imbalance on the top of a red bar and a market going sideways, is it important?

194
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:47,260
Yes, probably.

195
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,350
But it's more powerful when it's, you know, at the end of

196
00:13:50,350 --> 00:13:55,200
a move rather than just sort of stuck in the middle of a move on a market that's going nowhere.

197
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:59,551
You know, I always say you could have the greatest indicator in the world,

198
00:13:59,551 --> 00:14:02,659
but if it shoots off, you know, a perfect signal

199
00:14:02,659 --> 00:14:10,119
right in the middle of a trading range where the market's going nowhere, it's, you know, it's not going to be very lucrative trading.

200
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:19,050
So, you know, the next one would be an imbalance on the low, which is opposite to the imbalance on a high.

201
00:14:19,110 --> 00:14:21,510
It occurs when there's a selling imbalance at the bottom of a

202
00:14:21,510 --> 00:14:23,110
green bar. And what you want to do

203
00:14:23,110 --> 00:14:26,910
is you want to trade in the opposite direction. So in this case, it would be a buy signal.

204
00:14:26,910 --> 00:14:32,270
So you see here, you've got 1068 right on the low. It's a selling imbalance.

205
00:14:32,790 --> 00:14:38,870
Well, you know, people are being aggressive. They're selling it into the bid. Why isn't the market selling off more?

206
00:14:39,890 --> 00:14:43,977
Obviously, there was somebody in there with, you know, a strong bid,

207
00:14:43,977 --> 00:14:46,702
a strong passive bid to support the market

208
00:14:46,702 --> 00:14:50,449
that basically they're saying, you know, I'll take all you got.

209
00:14:50,450 --> 00:14:55,270
But, you know, they're not going to let the market go through that level. They may be defending the level.

210
00:14:56,210 --> 00:14:59,496
Now, imbalances on highs and lows are not going to

211
00:14:59,496 --> 00:15:02,125
be, you know, the end of trend, you

212
00:15:02,125 --> 00:15:08,369
know, or the high or the low of the day. You know, they happen in the course of trading.

213
00:15:08,770 --> 00:15:11,902
The market may come back to those levels later and, you know,

214
00:15:11,902 --> 00:15:13,990
and test it and get through those levels.

215
00:15:13,990 --> 00:15:18,949
You don't necessarily know if those participants are still there when the market circles around back to those levels.

216
00:15:18,950 --> 00:15:22,325
But for the short term, it's, you know, it's a

217
00:15:22,325 --> 00:15:25,025
nice little indicator to add to your cap,

218
00:15:25,025 --> 00:15:30,088
especially when you're, you know, you've got to think about what's happening to the market.

219
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:32,653
So, you have the market coming down. You

220
00:15:32,653 --> 00:15:35,096
have some very aggressive sellers right at the

221
00:15:35,096 --> 00:15:40,288
low. The market turns around. Market price action of the bar shows that there's buyers in there.

222
00:15:40,470 --> 00:15:44,490
You know, in this case, in this example, you've got a buying imbalance at the top end of the bar.

223
00:15:44,490 --> 00:15:50,130
So, you know, these people that sold earlier, you know, they're stuck. They've got to get out.

224
00:15:50,390 --> 00:15:54,230
You know, if they want to get out, they're going to add

225
00:15:54,230 --> 00:15:59,670
to the buying. But what's important is the buying couldn't follow through and that's where it turned.

